● <adaptive_rules>
  - Publication set outputs are printed line-by-line, ascending lexicographic order by identifier;
  empty set produces no output.
  - A Publication has a unique identifier (only lowercase letters and digits), a title, a
  publication year, and at least one author.
  - Author set outputs are printed line-by-line, ascending lexicographic order by last name (then
  first name); empty set produces no output.
  - Conference series and journals are uniquely identified by their type (series or journal) and
  their name; conferences are uniquely identified by conference series and year.
  - An Article can be published in exactly one publication venue (Conference or Journal).
  - <float> values are always output with exactly three decimal places in format X.XXX using '.' as
  separator, truncated (not rounded); values are <= 10^6.
  - Adhere to JavaDoc documentation rules.
  - After printing an error message, the program should continue and wait for the next input.
  - All whitespace rules must be followed.
  - Allowed Java library packages: java.lang, java.io, java.util, java.util.regex, java.util.stream,
   java.util.function unless otherwise stated.
  - Choose appropriate visibilities for classes, methods, and attributes.
  - Do not call System.exit(), Runtime.exit(), or similar.
  - Do not use the default package.
  - Error messages may be free-form English text but should be meaningful.
  - Error messages must begin with 'Error,' and must not contain special characters such as newlines
   or umlauts.
  - Error messages should make clear to the user why an input was rejected.
  - Follow rules for variable, method, and package naming.
  - For error messages, using System.err instead of System.out is allowed.
  - If user input does not match the required format, output an error message.
  - Interactive program output must exactly match the specification including case, spaces, and line
   breaks.
  - Maximum line width of 120 characters must be enforced.
  - Never reassign standard input or standard output streams.
  - On semantic or syntactic specification violations always output a meaningful error message.
  - Only produce the information specified in the task; do not print any additional information.
  - Program code must compile without errors.
  - Requires passing mandatory tests before other tests are evaluated; violation yields automatic
  zero points.
  - Solutions must use Java SE 17.
  - Some Checkstyle rules are specially marked and their violation causes automatic zero points.
  - Submit *.java files for task A in individual work with corresponding folder structure in the
  designated directory.
  - Use System.in for input unless otherwise stated.
  - Use System.out for output unless otherwise stated.
  - For a given year, at most one conference of a conference series may take place.
  - Keywords propagate automatically: from conference series to conferences, from conferences to
  articles presented there, and from journals to articles published there; duplicate keywords are
  counted/mentioned only once.
  - A Journal always has exactly one publisher.
  - Implementing quit must not use System.exit() or Runtime.exit().
  </adaptive_rules>

